# Line endings are load-bearing in this repository.
#
# `crates/l2-mods/rulesets/core/rules/*.toml` are *generated* documents, and
# `crates/l2-mods/tests/core.rs` compares them byte for byte against the
# renderer that produced them. The renderer emits "\n".
#
# GitHub's Windows runners default to `core.autocrlf=true`, so a fresh checkout
# there rewrote those files with CRLF and the comparison failed - green on
# Linux, red on Windows, and passing on every developer machine configured with
# `autocrlf=input`. Normalising here fixes it for every checkout on every
# platform, which a per-test workaround would not: a test that shrugged off
# line endings would also stop noticing real drift in the generated files.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is the part that matters - it pins the
# *working tree*, overriding whatever core.autocrlf the machine has.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts that people may open in tools which insist on CRLF.
# Nothing compares these byte-for-byte.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never touch these, whatever heuristics would otherwise guess.
# The asset types are gitignored and must never be committed (CLAUDE.md rule 1);
# they are listed anyway so that a force-add cannot silently corrupt one.
*.pl8  binary
*.256  binary
*.smk  binary
*.wav  binary
*.saf  binary
*.sav  binary
*.dat  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.png  binary
*.bmp  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.bundle binary

# **The symbol databases merge by key, never by line.**
#
# `docs/symbols.json` once produced nine conflict hunks in which the `ours` body
# of every hunk sat under the *wrong entry's* `name` — git had aligned two
# arrays that were ordered differently, so the diff paired unrelated records and
# offered `County_PlaceBlacksmith`'s comment as a candidate body for
# `Move_BuildCostMap`. Nothing was wrong with the data: the two branches had
# touched different addresses and only the order differed. A textual resolution
# would have produced a symbol database that parses, reads plausibly, and lies.
#
# What caught it was `JSON.parse` failing on the union — a syntactic check
# catching a semantic disaster by luck, which is not a defence to rely on twice.
# `docs/agents.md`, *A file that looks like data is usually a claim*.
#
# The driver also honours a DELETION on one side, which is what
# `docs/hypotheses.json` needs: promotion is a move, not a copy, and a naive
# union turns every promotion back into a copy — it did, four times, in one
# evening.
#
# `merge=l2json` needs the driver registered per clone; git will not run a
# driver a repository merely asks for. `tools/symbols/install-merge-driver.sh`
# does it. WITHOUT that registration git falls back to the ordinary text merge,
# which is safe because it is loud: the conflict markers appear as before.
docs/symbols.json    merge=l2json
docs/hypotheses.json merge=l2json
docs/records.json    merge=l2json
